"""Group micro-lesson data: one dedicated courseware packet of about 15 minutes for each of the 33 groups in the category index.

Each entry: lesson title | why this group of entries exists | observation points [(term, point) x 3-4] | exercise | acceptance check | self-check question | correct option | wrong option | feedback.
Positioning: bridge the gap between the "shared category course" and the "category entries" — this is not a full specialty course; it gives each group of entries a targeted method of entry. Full specialty training still requires on-site conditions.
"""

MICRO={
('00','History and Theory'):('Theory Is a Toolbox for Asking Questions','The entries in this group all answer one question: how do we ask? Art history asks when, where, and how a work was used; aesthetics asks where artistic value comes from; criticism asks whether the work holds up under specific criteria. Theory is not jargon to be applied; it is a way of choosing evidence.',[('Historiography of art history','The discipline\'s own history: why did "the Renaissance" enter the general textbooks before "Ming painting"? Whoever wrote the first textbook set the default point of view.'),('Iconography','From "I see a Madonna" to "why this Madonna": interpretation must rest on accurate description checked against cultural conventions.'),('Comparative art studies','Comparison needs a shared question, not a ranking: first ask "what problem is each of them solving," then speak of similarities and differences.'),('The four theories of art','The theory of imitation (mimesis) asks whether art re-presents the world; expression theory asks whether it expresses emotion; formalism asks whether form itself carries meaning; the institutional theory of art asks how the art world recognizes an object — the same work will be asked four different questions.')],'Choose one work and write one question in each of three modes — art history, aesthetics, criticism: when, where, and by whom was it used / on what grounds does it hold together / how does it measure up under my criteria.','The three questions each do their own job; none substitutes for or blurs into another.','What is usually the first step of iconographic analysis?','Accurately describing the visible content first','Asserting symbolic meaning outright','Panofsky\'s pre-iconographic description comes before interpretation: see clearly first, then interpret.'),
('00','People and Institutions'):('Who Pays, Who Watches, Who Defines','The shared question of this group: art is not made in a vacuum. Commissioning, education, dissemination, management, and policy together decide what gets made, what gets seen, and what gets remembered.',[('Sociology of art','Taste has social coordinates: who has leisure, who has education, and who enters the gallery all shape the default answer to "what is good art."'),('Art market research','Price and value are often out of alignment: scarcity, auction history, and institutional authentication constitute a logic of their own.'),('Cultural policy','An intangible-heritage list both protects and reshapes: once an item is listed, the form of its transmission also changes.')],'Look up a work you view often and write down four questions: who made it, who funded it, who had the right to view it at the time, and who can see it now.','All four questions have sources; for anything you cannot find, write "unknown" plainly.','What evidence does research in the sociology of art need first?','Social records of making and viewing','Psychological passages in the artist\'s autobiography','Social position requires institutional evidence; psychological conjecture cannot take its place.'),
('00','Collecting and Conservation'):('Collections Are the Result of Choices','A museum is not a neutral warehouse. The history of collecting and provenance research ask how the objects came to be here; conservation decides how long they remain and in what condition.',[('Provenance research','Provenance: gaps in the chain of transmission are themselves historical evidence. A gap is not a scandal, but it must be flagged.'),('Preventive conservation','Controlling temperature, humidity, and light comes ahead of restoration intervention: leaving the object alone is often the most professional decision.'),('Digital preservation','Format migration and its relation to the "original": a scan cannot replace the physical object, but it can replace part of access.')],'On any museum website, find the "provenance / acquisition" information for one object and record the murkiest segment of the chain.','Point to at least one gap, neither exaggerating nor concealing it.','What does "preventive conservation" do first?','Control environmental conditions such as temperature, humidity, and light','Restore objects to look as new as possible','Prevention before intervention is the baseline of conservation ethics.'),
('01','Materials and Techniques'):('Identifying the Medium from Its Traces','Each material leaves a distinct boundary of trace and a distinct way of being revised. Recognizing the medium is the first step of formal analysis and the foundation for the idea that the material speaks too.',[('Drawing and sketching','The visibility of revision: eraser marks and hesitations are themselves a record of time.'),('Ink painting','The time window of water and paper: because ink cannot be covered over, the sequence of writing becomes visible evidence.'),('Acrylic and oil painting','Drying speed dictates the layering strategy: fast-drying media suit direct overpainting; slow-drying media allow long blending.')],'Find three images in different media (one ink painting, one oil painting, one drawing) and point out in each a trace that "only this material could leave."','The trace descriptions are identifiable and re-checkable, without adjectives like "more spirited."','What does the non-coverability of ink mainly produce?','The sequence of writing becomes visible evidence','The picture is necessarily of a higher order','A material\'s limits shape method; they do not create a hierarchy of value.'),
('01','Printmaking and Publishing'):('The Matrix Is the Mother of Multiples','A print\'s image comes from the contact of the matrix with paper. Plate wear, edition numbers, and signing conventions all remind you: what you see is one impression, not a unique original.',[('The four print processes','Relief (woodcut) and intaglio (engraving and etching) run on opposite black-and-white logic: one cuts away the white and keeps the black, the other bites recesses that hold the ink; lithography images through the mutual repulsion of oil and water; screenprint pushes ink through the open mesh by squeegee pressure — the process decides the character of the impression and of the multiple.'),('Edition number and plate wear','Number 3/50 and number 49/50 may differ visibly in state: the edition number is both a system of honesty and a mechanism of value.'),('Artists\' books','The book as a work, not a container: page order, layout, and binding are all creative decisions.')],'Find a print in any collection and record its process and edition information; if either is missing, write "unknown" and list the places you checked.','You can keep description of the process technique separate from description of the image content.','Why may two impressions of the same plate differ?','Printing conditions and plate wear change','Prints are all exactly identical copies','Printmaking is a process medium, and the edition system is precisely a response to that.'),
('01','Pictorial Questions'):('Subject Matter Is a Question, Not a List','A portrait is never only "a good likeness": it handles identity, commemoration, and power. Shanshui (landscape) painting and Western landscape painting are two programs for viewing nature; still life turns "things" into the theme.',[('Shanshui and landscape','Wandering viewing versus the fixed point: the moving viewpoint of the handscroll and fixed-viewpoint perspective are two spatial rhetorics.'),('Portraiture','The social function beyond likeness: who was portrayed, what they wear, and where they look are all statements of identity.'),('History painting','The choice of narrative climax: which instant is painted decides which layer of the story the viewer sees.')],'Choose one portrait and one shanshui (or landscape) and answer for each: "what does it want the viewer to do?"','Evidence comes from composition, format, and orientation; do not answer from the artist\'s psychology.','What is the main difference between landscape painting and shanshui painting?','They belong to different traditions of viewing and organizing space','One is realistic and the other expressive','The "realistic / expressive" split conceals the real difference between two systems of space.'),
('02','Script Styles'):('Five Coexisting Scripts, Not a Relay','Script evolution is often told as a straight line "from seal script to cursive, ever more convenient"; in fact the styles coexisted in functional layers: solemn occasions used formal scripts, daily writing used cursive, and every style stayed alive in every period.',[('Formal and cursive scripts','Stele carvings use formal scripts, letters use running and cursive: the occasion decides the script, and the script in turn marks the occasion.'),('The clerical transformation (libian)','The structural turn of Chinese characters from seal script toward clerical script: conversion into strokes and simplification is an event in the history of writing, not an aesthetic movement.'),('Cursive script','The barrier to reading is real: it is a conventional system for high-speed writing, not arbitrary marks.'),('Recognizing the five script styles','Seal script is even-textured and elongated; clerical script carries the wavelike flaring stroke ("silkworm head, swallow tail"); regular script is squared; running script links strokes; cursive script turns them into signs: recognize the forms first, discuss style afterward.')],'Find one work in seal script and one in regular (or running) script; record in each one structural feature that exists "for speed" or "for solemnity."','Features are stated in terms of strokes and structure, and the two works are compared.','What does "the clerical transformation (libian)" refer to?','The structural transformation of Chinese characters from seal script to clerical script','An aesthetic movement to write characters more neatly','The clerical transformation changed the basic construction of Chinese characters; it precedes any judgment of style.'),
('02','Research and Practice'):('Copying Is Analysis, Not Photocopying','These entries form a methodology of study: a hierarchy of observation running from brushwork to overall composition, the relation between copying and assembling characters, and the two great traditions of study, steles and modelbooks.',[('Analytical copying','Extract only one problem per pass: this pass watches only the turns, the next only the spacing.'),('Assembling characters (jizi)','An exercise in organizing a new composition without the context of the original: it trains judgment of relations, not single characters.'),('Stele studies and modelbook studies','The mediating difference between carved marks and ink traces: a stele\'s square crispness may come from the knife and from weathering, not from the brush.')],'Choose a model of five characters; make a spatial analysis diagram first, then copy it once, and write two notes on the deviations.','Deviations are described down to relations between concrete components — not as "lacking spirit resonance."','What is the key to analytical copying?','Extracting and testing only one problem per pass','Making it as identical to the model as possible','The aim is to understand the method, not to reproduce the look.'),
('03','Forms and Media'):('Form Dictates How It Is Viewed','Sculpture in the round demands circling, relief depends on the frontal view, openwork makes negative space the protagonist. Form here is not a classification label; it prescribes how the body takes part in viewing.',[('Compression in relief','Depth is translated into layers: low relief works through edge lines, high relief through real rise and fall.'),('Openwork and negative space','The void becomes form: once the material is pierced, the silhouette keeps changing with the light behind it.'),('The readymade','Form yields to context: the same urinal is a different object in a hardware store and in a gallery.')],'In paper, make one relief version and one openwork version of the same object, and record how the way of viewing changes.','The conclusion compares at least two directions of viewing.','What is the most direct difference between sculpture in the round and relief?','They presuppose different viewing paths','Sculpture in the round is necessarily more artistic','Form prescribes how the body and the work move in relation to each other.'),
('03','Space and Events'):('Space and Time Become Materials','These practices turn "where, how long, who takes part" into parameters of the work; documentation and reperformance therefore become central questions.',[('Site-specific art','Made for this place: after relocation, even an exact rebuilding faces an altered web of context.'),('Socially engaged art','The ethical weight of "relations" as medium: participants are not material, and the right to withdraw must genuinely exist.'),('Land art','Inaccessibility challenges the exhibition system: most people reach the work only through photographs and maps.')],'Design a "one-minute event" for a corner you know well: write the rules, the audience position, and how it ends; do not stage it.','The rules are executable, and the audience role and the way out are explicit.','What usually happens when a site-specific work moves into a museum?','The context changes, and the character of the work changes with it','It remains entirely unaffected','The site is a parameter of the work, not a backdrop.'),
('04','Spatial Scales'):('A Continuous Scale from Room to City','The handle at the door, the room, the street, the city: the same bodily experience at different scales; thinking across scales is the shared training of this group.',[('Vernacular architecture','Architecture without architects: the long trial-and-error product of convention, climate, and local materials.'),('Adaptive reuse','Old structure, new use: what to keep and what to alter — each item is a contestable decision.'),('Public space','Who is welcome to stay: seating, shade, and accessibility decide who actually uses the space.')],'Draw a route you walk often, mark three points where the scale breaks (e.g., from indoors to an alley mouth to a square), and record how your bodily sense changes.','Each break point carries one observable piece of evidence.','What does the study of "vernacular architecture" remind us of?','Architectural knowledge also lives in nonprofessional traditions of design','Vernacular simply means more backward','It dissolves the hierarchy of professional over folk knowledge: first see how a building answers its conditions.'),
('04','Dimensions of Observation'):('Learning the Vocabulary for Reading Drawings','Plan, section, and elevation are three ways of cutting, each with places it cannot reach; circulation, light, sound, and climate are evidence from the bodily dimension.',[('Section','Height relations the plan cannot show: the difference in light distribution between a clerestory and a side window is seen in section.'),('Acoustic environment','Surface materials decide reverberation: hard walls, soft upholstery, and an acoustical ceiling are three sound solutions.'),('Climate response','Deep eaves and orientation are at once form and strategy: a history of climate is stored in form.')],'Find a plan of a room (or draw one), add a section, and annotate the direction of light and one acoustic feature.','The drawing and on-site observation can be checked against each other.','What does a section add most directly to a plan?','Height, layering, and vertical relations','Furniture and color schemes','Every drawing is a trade-off; the section carries the vertical relations.'),
('05','Craft Materials'):('The Temper of Materials and the Working Process','Every material has its windows of shrinkage, stretch, fusion, and drying; the working process is a negotiation that converts temper into form.',[('Ceramics','The chained variables of clay, glaze, and fire: a deviation in one stage surfaces in the next.'),('Lacquer art','Curing that depends on humidity: raw lacquer polymerizes into film in a moist environment — a slow material shapes a slow craft.'),('Weaving','The mathematics of warp and weft: whether a pattern is feasible is decided by the structure of the loom, not by what one can draw.'),('Metalwork, enamel, and glass','Forging shapes by hammering; casting fills the mold with molten metal; enamel is a glassy fusion onto a metal body; glass depends on blowing and kiln casting. Each material has its own thermal window, tools, and traces; in observation, first assign each trace to its craft.')],'Take samples of two materials (paper and cloth are enough), fold the same design in both, and record where each failed and your hypothesis about why.','Failure is recorded as information about the process, not as "clumsy hands."','What does the curing of lacquer mainly depend on in its environment?','Humidity','Direct sunlight','Raw lacquer needs a moist environment to polymerize into film, and that directly shapes the rhythm of the craft.'),
('05','Design Fields'):('Design Is Defining the Problem','What the entries in this group share: situation and task come first, form comes after. Style is a result, not a point of departure.',[('Information design','Testing hierarchy against misreading: whether the key information can be found within five seconds is a hard metric.'),('Service design','The touchpoint map: experience is organized across many points of contact in a process, not only in the moment of use.'),('Speculative design','Asking through "what if": it does not seek implementation; it seeks to open a space for discussion.'),('Fashion and textiles','Design tasks fitted to the body: cut pattern, fabric, and how a garment goes on and off jointly decide function; "fashion" is only one layer of it.')],'Write a testable design brief: who, in what situation, performs what action, and how success is judged.','The success criteria are observable and do not depend on adjectives.','What is the most important part of a design brief?','The situation of use and the criteria of judgment','A description of the visual style','Without task and criteria, there is nothing by which to evaluate style.'),
('06','Genres and Uses'):('Genre Is a Mode of Use','"Documentary" is not a style but a promise about use: caption, source, and shooting conditions constitute its chain of evidence. Staged and experimental work is not false, but it requires a different honesty.',[('Documentary','The chain of evidence: who shot it, when, where, and under what conditions — lose one item and the use changes.'),('Scientific imaging','An aesthetics in the service of measurement: scale bar, light source, and waveband may never be omitted from the labeling.'),('Conceptual photography','Is the photograph a prop for the concept or its subject? The answer decides the standard of evaluation.')],'Write three captions for the same photograph — documentary, commercial, conceptual — and compare what each promises.','You can point out what remains "unverified" in each caption.','Where does the "evidential" character of documentary photography mainly come from?','Checkable shooting conditions and a chain of sources','The texture of black-and-white grain','Evidence lies in institutions and records, not in texture.'),
('06','Technology and Presentation'):('The Presentation Chain from Darkroom to Screen','The same negative is three objects — in the darkroom, on the screen, and in the book; the presentation chain is a constituent of the work.',[('Darkroom','Local dodging and burning: to develop is to interpret, and every enlargement is a decision.'),('Photobook','Rhythm designed through sequence and page turn: the temporality of the book is governed by the speed of the reader\'s hand.'),('Photographic archives','Institutional custody decides visibility: accessioning, cataloging, and digitization each have their own threshold.')],'Shoot the same scene three times on a phone; adjust the presentation for screen, print, and projection, and record how the "best" one changes with the medium.','The conclusion clearly separates the "file" from the "presentation."','Why is a negative not yet the work?','The medium and process of presentation take part in the final effect','The negative is worthless','From printing parameters to the medium of display, every step is a creative decision.'),
('07','Literary Genres'):('Genre Is a Frame of Expectation','Readers enter a text with generic expectations: the "once upon a time" of the fairy tale, the source-pressure of reportage, the serial rhythm of web fiction. Genre is a contract, and it can also take effect by being broken.',[('Memoir and biography','The slant of first-person memory against the archival duty of the third person: two ways of being honest.'),('Reportage','Literary technique plus verifiability: once the checking is lost, only sentiment is left.'),('Serialization','The end-of-chapter hook shapes the structure: the rhythm of waiting itself is part of the form.')],'Rewrite a 200-word prose passage as the opening of a fairy tale and as the opening of reportage, keeping the core of fact intact in both.','The layer of fact is identical in the two versions.','How does knowledge of genre help reading?','It supplies a frame of expectation against which violations register','Genre decides whether a work is good or bad','Genre is a contract formed by history, not a label of quality.'),
('07','Narrative and Language'):('The Movable Parts of Narrative','Point of view, time, and structure are parts that can be taken out and tested separately; change one, and all the rest carry the load differently.',[('Point of view','The allocation of information rights: who knows what, and when, is the mechanism of all suspense.'),('Rhythm','The design of time in the layer of sound: why does this sentence read fast? Sentence length and punctuation are governing the breath.'),('Literature in translation','Every choice the translator makes is an interpretation: to compare two translations is to compare two readings.')],'Take a 300-word narrative, rewrite it changing only the point of view, and list the gains and losses of information.','Each gain or loss is matched to the change of viewpoint.','What is the most basic function of "point of view"?','Allocating what the reader may know','Announcing the author\'s political position','Point of view is a device for allocating information, not a device for declaring allegiance.'),
('08','Creation and Practice'):('A Continuum from Voicing to Design','Sound practice runs continuously from the body through instruments to circuits and the field; the shared ground is the organization of time and the listener\'s attention.',[('Improvisation','The grammar of real-time decision: good improvisation has vocabulary, has response, and knows how to give something up.'),('Recording production','Microphone placement is interpretation: the sense of space and the balance are half decided before recording is pressed.'),('Soundscape','The environment as the work: listening itself can be trained and organized.')],'Record one minute of environmental sound, mark the time points of three events, and organize them into three timelines of "opening — turn — close."','The time points can be checked by listening again.','What does "microphone placement" affect in recording production?','The interpretation of sense of space and balance','It is only a technical detail, unrelated to the music','Medium and placement are formal decisions in a work of sound.'),
('08','Dimensions of Study'):('Aural Analysis, Compartment by Compartment','Rhythm, melody, harmony, and musical form are distinct layers of analysis; listening in compartments is the method that turns a confused soundscape into an object that can be discussed.',[('Interval','The color of distance: consonance and dissonance are relative; tension and release are what drive the music.'),('Polyphony','A rule system in which the voices converse as equals: the fugue is the game of taking those rules to their extreme.'),('Ethnomusicology','Putting "music theory" in relative view: the tunings, notations, and ways of training of different traditions cannot be applied to one another.')],'Choose 30 seconds of music; listen three times — once only for rhythm, once only for melody, once only for the bass line — and draw a timeline for each.','The three lines can be aligned on one time axis.','What is the purpose of "layered listening"?','To break what is heard into layers that can be discussed','To take the pleasure out of the music','Analysis is temporary scaffolding; listening remains an experience of the whole.'),
('08','Traditions and Genres'):('The History of Genres Is Not an Evolution','The swing of jazz, the timbre synthesis of electronic music, the yin-nao ornaments (vibrato and portamento) of the guqin (seven-string zither): each tradition has its own core parameters, and one ruler cannot rank them all.',[('Jazz','Swing feel and the grammar of harmonic improvisation: swing comes from the uneven division of eighth notes and the steady groove of the rhythm section, not from rushing.'),('Electronic music','Synthesis parameters become the "orchestration" directly: filter and envelope are the playing.'),('Oral transmission in traditional music','The tension between oral transmission and notation: before recordings, the very notion of a "version" was different.')],'Listen to one minute each of two different traditions; for each, write down "the one most important parameter" and give time-point evidence.','The two parameters differ and each carries evidence; "both sound lovely" does not count.','What is the starting point for comparing different musical traditions?','Finding the constitutive parameters inside each context','Ranking them by complexity','Comparison needs standards inside both contexts, not an outside ruler.'),
('09','Practices and Traditions'):('Genealogies of Bodily Technique','The open, stretched, upright line of ballet, the shenyun (body rhythm) of Chinese classical dance, the groove of street dance — each trains a different set of bodily defaults; when watching, first identify "what this body is working to solve."',[('Ballet','Systematizing the vertical axis and turnout: the center of weight is trained onto a precise, narrow band.'),('Street dance','Groove comes before a list of steps: a continuous response to the music comes first, steps after.'),('Contact improvisation','An improvised grammar of weight and shared points of balance: trust is a technique, not an attitude.'),('Folk and ethnic dance','Take the courtyard figures and gong-and-drum cues of yangge (folk dance): gait, formation, and instruments together compose a local body memory; when watching, first identify "what this body is working to solve."')],'Watch one minute each of two dance traditions; in each, record three "bodily defaults": center of weight, way of initiating force, relation to the music.','The description stays with facts of the body, without remarks like "more elegant."','What does one look at first to identify a dance tradition?','How the body is organized and how it initiates force','Whether the costumes are splendid','The lineage is written in the body, not in the scenery.'),
('09','Creation and Research'):('Choreography Organizes Variables','Body, space, time, and effort are the four faders of choreography; a good work is not every fader wide open, but clear relations within limited parameters.',[('Effort (dynamics)','Effort: the same movement with a different energy is a different syntax — light and heavy are language, not decoration.'),('Dance notation','The cost of turning movement into preservable notation: what is written down, and what is lost.'),('Dance film/video','Choreography of the camera: camera position and editing become part of the body.')],'Make three versions of an eight-count phrase changing only "effort," and ask viewers to tell the versions apart and rank them.','The differences among the three versions are recognized reliably by others.','What is the value of "constraint" in choreography?','It makes relations perceivable and comparable','Constraint inevitably obstructs expression','Only within limited variables does a readable structure emerge.'),
('10','Theatre and the Integrated Stage'):('The Leading Medium of the Integrated Stage','Opera is led by music, dance drama by the body, puppetry by the object; "integrated" is not the adding of elements but the choice of a leading medium.',[('Puppetry','The soul of the object: whether the puppeteer\'s rhythm of breath is visible decides the puppet\'s sense of life.'),('Immersive theatre','The audience becomes a variable of space: the freedom to move is both a gift and a burden.'),('Applied theatre','Theater tools applied in educational and community settings: the goal has changed, so the standard of evaluation must change as well.')],'Take the core of one story and write one sentence each for opera, puppetry, and immersive theatre on "how the leading medium tells it."','Each of the three proposals points to the distinctive capacity of its leading medium.','What is the key judgment about an "integrated art"?','Which medium leads and organizes the other elements','The more elements, the better','The leading medium decides the logic of the structure; it is not addition.'),
('10','Xiqu and Quyi'):('Conventions Are Compressed Language','Qiba (armoring the general), tangma (riding the horse), water sleeves: conventions compress complex experience into units that can be learned and recombined; understanding conventions is like understanding grammar, not memorizing words.',[('Role categories (hangdang)','A typed system of acting, voice, and stage appearance: the role category is an entrance, not a cage.'),('Narrative in quyi','The craft, in quyi (narrative telling and singing), of one teller carrying many roles: the comedic "bundles" (baofu) of pingshu storytelling are precision designs in timing.'),('The shuimo ("water-polished") style','A strict correspondence of syllable sound and melody: setting the tune by the word sounds keeps the sung text always clear.')],'Watch a passage of Peking opera or Kunqu opera; identify one conventionalized action, and write down what experience it compresses and what fundamental training it demands.','The identification reaches a concrete action and role category, not loose talk of "national treasure."','What is the right way to understand xiqu conventions?','A performance vocabulary that can be learned and recombined','Old rules that never change','Conventions are the grammar of a history still generating new sentences.'),
('10','Live Performance and Collaboration'):('How an Evening of Performance Is Organized','From script to stage management, the theater is a production chain; the prompt book records the contract of each collaboration.',[('Stage management','Cues: the exact handoffs of light, sound, and scenery — the cue sheet is the score of the collaboration.'),('Stage design','The assumed character of space: one table and two chairs, and the revolving stage, are two different proposals for a world.'),('Performance art','The risk and unrepeatable character of liveness: there is no safety net of "running the show again."')],'Write a cue sheet for a two-minute scene: at least three lighting or sound change points, each annotated with its trigger and purpose.','Every cue has a trigger signal and a clear dramatic function.','What is a cue sheet for?','It lets each department hand off at the exact moment','It is a decorative piece of paperwork','It is the carrier of precision in collaborative art.'),
('11','Audiovisual Forms'):('Form Comes from the Context of Screening','Theater, television, gallery, phone: the context of screening shapes the length and rhythm of forms; video art and the essay film have each redefined the contract of "watching."',[('Essay film','First-person reflection and the collage of materials: the image as argument rather than story.'),('Video art','Loop and juxtaposition replace the linear: viewers enter at any moment, and the work has no "beginning."'),('Online audiovisual media','The vertical format and the first three seconds of attention: form is shaped in return by the structure of distribution.')],'Develop one event into three forms — a 90-second vertical video, a 10-minute documentary, a gallery loop installation — and write one sentence of structural strategy for each.','The formal decisions of the three versions answer their contexts; they are not merely different lengths.','What fundamentally separates video art from the narrative film?','A different context of display and structure of time','Cheaper equipment','The gallery context changes the narrative contract: you may enter midway and leave at any time.'),
('11','Animation'):('A Frame-by-Frame Worldview','Animation is not "pictures that move": it constructs time frame by frame, and physical law is a choice rather than a default.',[('Stop motion','The tactile time of materials: every twenty-fourth of a second must be set by hand.'),('Paper-cut animation','Flatness joined with the fine-arts tradition: the path of the Shanghai Animation Film Studio repays study.'),('Relations between frames','Anticipation, action, follow-through: the illusion of movement comes from the design of relations between frame and frame.')],'Make a 12-frame flip-book animation that shows only the three-part rhythm of "anticipation — action — follow-through"; do not try to draw well.','Viewers can recognize the three-part rhythm.','What is the core ontology of animation?','Constructing time and movement frame by frame','Drawing that looks like real people','The illusion of movement comes from deliberate design between frames, not from lifelikeness.'),
('11','Production and Research'):('Departmental Languages and Overall Judgment','Every department has its own grammar: the cut point, the grading range, the layers of the mix; the director\'s work is to make trade-offs among departmental languages.',[('Storyboard','Previsualization — and when to set the storyboard aside and leave room for discovery on the spot.'),('Color grading','The chemistry of mood: its fit with the art and lighting departments decides the logic of the overall palette.'),('Film preservation','Prints and versions: of which year\'s restoration is the "old film" you are watching?')],'Choose one scene and analyze only where the cut points fall in the action (during the action / after the action); write down three regularities.','The regularities carry time points and can be tested against counterexamples.','What does a "restored version" remind us of?','A film is an object on a chain of versions','Once restored, it is the original','The history of preservation and restoration is itself part of film history.'),
('12','Interaction and Computation'):('Rules, Data, and Emergence','The distinctive trait of computational media: behavior is produced by rules in real time; the work is a system, not a fixed object.',[('Generative art','A rule system produces the outcome, with randomness only an optional ingredient: a system without randomness can still be generative art; selection and combination are authorial acts, so keep a process archive.'),('Data art','The choice of data is already a stance: what is chosen and what is left out are both editing.'),('AI art','The process archive: tools, prompts, batches, selections, and revisions must all be traceable before responsibility can be discussed.')],'Write an "if... then..." rule system (paper is enough), run it three times, and record which run produced a result your design did not foresee.','You can tell "designed behavior" from "emergent behavior."','What does "emergence" mean in computational art?','Rule interactions produce behavior never explicitly written','The picture looks more complicated','Emergence is a property of systems, not a density of ornament.'),
('12','Games and Immersion'):('Mechanics Are Rhetoric','Rules of play carry values: scarce resources teach trade-offs, the cost of failure teaches caution; "mechanics are rhetoric" is the key to this group of entries.',[('Level design','Teaching without words: a good first three minutes teaches the basic controls without any manual.'),('Serious games','Goals turned outward: training and educational games cannot be judged by the metrics of entertainment.'),('Virtual reality','Embodiment and motion sickness: the body becomes the interface, and comfort is a design floor, not a concession.'),('Game genres','Clusters of rule conventions — platformer, role-playing, strategy, shooter: genre is shorthand for a design tradition, and innovation often happens where genres cross.')],'Analyze a game you know well: write down one "value" that a mechanic conveys, then design a change that makes it convey the opposite value.','The change happens at the level of rules, not at the level of copy.','What does "mechanics are rhetoric" mean?','Rules and their consequences convey claims to the player','Games should talk players into buying things','The structure of the system itself speaks, and the player receives it in play.'),
('13','Art in Everyday Life'):('Use Comes Before Display','Paper-cut window flowers are pasted on windows, masks are worn on faces: the first site of these forms is use, not the showcase; materials and craft obey the needs of the occasion.',[('New Year prints (nianhua)','Solar terms and door gods: the calendar of the year shapes the subjects, and the place of pasting shapes the composition; one block prints one color, and the count of blocks is a clue to the craft.'),('Lanterns','An engineering of light, structure, and moving by night: translucency and the bearing of weight are real physical problems.'),('Folk narrative','The fluidity of the oral version: there is no single "definitive text," and the variant tellings are themselves historical evidence.')],'Document one festive object in your home: who uses it, when, and where it goes afterward; add an account of its provenance and note whether it is firsthand or hearsay.','You distinguish the functional chain of the object from the evidential grade of its provenance story.','Why does folk art research stress the "first site"?','The occasion of use determines form and meaning','Once inside a gallery it loses its value','Context is not background; it is a constituent part of the work.'),
('13','Intermedia Practices'):('The Boundary Is the Material','These practices take the boundary between media as their material: edible, smellable, to be lived with; to criticize them one returns to rules and experience, instead of asking first "is this art."',[('Conceptual art','The division of labor between execution and idea: the copyright and the re-execution of instruction works are real problems.'),('Bio art','The ethics and the duty of care for living systems: if the work is alive, the author owes it care.'),('Cross-sensory practice','Synesthesia is not spectacle: how multiple sensory channels calibrate one another is the real question.')],'Design a one-minute "single sense" experience (smell only, or touch only); write the rules, the expected difficulties, and an exit mechanism.','The rules restrict the mode of perception and provide for the participant\'s right to withdraw.','What is the starting point for evaluating an intermedia work?','Understanding its rules and the design of the experience','Asking first whether it counts as art','Begin with "how it works"; place the judgment of value after the evidence.'),
}
